        <p>Sinharaja Forest Reserve is Sri Lanka's most important patch of wilderness — the last substantial area of primary lowland rainforest remaining on the island. It was declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1988 in recognition of its extraordinary biodiversity: over 60% of the trees found here are endemic to Sri Lanka and found nowhere else on Earth, and the forest harbours the highest concentration of endemic bird species in the country. For serious bird watchers, Sinharaja is one of the most rewarding destinations in Asia. For everyone else, it is simply one of the most atmospheric places in Sri Lanka — a place where the forest closes around you and the noise of the outside world disappears entirely.</p>

        <h2>Why Sinharaja Matters</h2>
        <p>Sri Lanka was once almost entirely forested. Centuries of settlement, agriculture, and colonial-era plantation development stripped the lowland rainforest to a fraction of its original extent. Sinharaja is what remains — a 11,187-hectare remnant of what the island's interior once looked like everywhere. The forest is not simply valuable as a tourist attraction: it is a critical watershed, supplying water to 12 rivers and streams that serve communities across the south and southwest of the island. It is also a living seed bank for plant species that exist nowhere else.</p>
        <p>The name Sinharaja means "Lion King" in Sinhala — a reference to the legendary founding king of the Sinhalese people, whose kingdom, according to myth, began in this forest. The reserve's boundaries have been contested and encroached upon repeatedly over the past century, but it remains legally protected and a Sri Lanka Forest Department permit is required to enter.</p>

        <h2>Birds of Sinharaja</h2>
        <p>Sinharaja is the finest bird watching destination in Sri Lanka and one of the top sites in South Asia. The forest is home to 147 bird species of which 26 are endemic to Sri Lanka — and remarkably, most of these endemics can be seen in a single morning visit with a good guide. The bird diversity is concentrated partly because of a remarkable local behaviour: Sinharaja's endemic species form large mixed-species feeding flocks that move through the forest together, sometimes containing 10–15 different species at once. Following one of these flocks through the undergrowth is one of the great wildlife experiences in Sri Lanka.</p>
        <p>Key species to look for include the Sri Lanka blue magpie (one of the most striking birds in Asia), the red-faced malkoha, the green-billed coucal, the chestnut-backed owlet, the Sri Lanka spurfowl, the ashy-headed laughingthrush, and the white-faced starling. The Sri Lanka crested drongo often leads the feeding flocks, acting as a sentinel. Come at dawn — activity drops significantly after 9am as the forest heats up.</p>

        <h2>Other Wildlife</h2>
        <p>Beyond birds, Sinharaja has exceptional reptile diversity — 45 species of reptile are found here, of which many are endemic, including several species of gecko, skink and snake discovered only in the past few decades. The purple-faced langur monkey is frequently seen in the canopy — noisy, acrobatic and curious about visitors. The rare and shy red slender loris (a small nocturnal primate) lives in Sinharaja but is almost never seen without a specialist night tour. Leopards and elephants are technically present in the peripheral buffer zone but sightings are extremely rare.</p>
        <p>The forest floor is extraordinarily rich in invertebrates — giant millipedes, land crabs, extraordinary stick insects, and (in wet season) leeches in significant numbers. Leech socks and DEET applied to footwear are essential. Butterflies are abundant throughout: Sinharaja has 21 endemic butterfly species.</p>

        <h2>Trekking in Sinharaja</h2>
        <p>All visitors to Sinharaja must be accompanied by a registered guide — solo entry is not permitted. This is both a conservation measure and a practical necessity: the forest has no marked trails and orientation is extremely difficult without experience. Guides are available at the main entry points (Kudawa, Deniyaya and Pitadeniya) and must be booked in advance, particularly at weekends when demand from domestic visitors is high.</p>
        <p>The most common trek follows the trail from the Kudawa entrance to the Moulawella Peak — a half-day walk of about 3–4 hours that passes through several forest types and offers the best chance of encountering the endemic bird flocks. A longer full-day route continues to the Sinhagala Peak (742 metres), the highest point within the reserve, with panoramic views across the forest canopy and surrounding hills when visibility is clear. Both routes require a reasonable level of fitness — the terrain is uneven, often muddy, and steep in places.</p>
        <p>There are no facilities inside the forest — no shops, toilets or rest areas. Bring everything you need: water (at least 2 litres per person), snacks, rain gear (even in the dry season the forest creates its own microclimate and showers are common), and sturdy closed-toe shoes.</p>

