        <h2>Entry Permits and Fees</h2>
        <p>Entry to Sinharaja requires a permit from the Forest Department, payable at the entrance. Fees as of 2025:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Foreign adults:</strong> approximately LKR 1,500–2,000 per person</li>
          <li><strong>Foreign children:</strong> approximately LKR 750–1,000</li>
          <li><strong>Guide fee:</strong> approximately LKR 2,000–3,500 per group per half-day (mandatory)</li>
          <li><strong>Video camera fee:</strong> additional charge applies if bringing professional equipment</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Fees are subject to change — check with your accommodation or the Forest Department (forestdept.gov.lk) for current rates. Weekends and public holidays see the highest visitor numbers; weekday visits are quieter and wildlife encounters tend to be better.</p>

        <h2>Best Time to Visit</h2>
        <p>Sinharaja has two distinct dry seasons: <strong>January to April</strong> and <strong>August to September</strong>. These are the best times to visit — trails are more manageable, leeches less abundant, and bird activity at its highest. The inter-monsoon periods (May and October–November) can bring heavy rain that makes the forest trails slippery and the experience uncomfortable.</p>
        <p>That said, Sinharaja receives rain year-round — it is a rainforest, and even in the dry season morning mist and afternoon showers are common. The forest after rain is extraordinarily beautiful, with the canopy dripping and light filtering through in golden columns. Many serious bird watchers prefer the damp conditions as birds are more active after rain.</p>

        <h2>Getting to Sinharaja</h2>
        <p>Sinharaja has three main entrance points:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Kudawa (north):</strong> The most popular entrance, accessed from Weddagala village near Kalawana. From Colombo: take the highway south toward Ratnapura, then Kalawana. Journey time: 3–3.5 hours. This is the main entrance used by most tour operators.</li>
          <li><strong>Deniyaya (south):</strong> The southern entrance, accessed from Deniyaya town on the Matara–Rakwana road. More convenient if approaching from Galle or Matara (approximately 2 hours from Galle). Fewer visitors use this entrance, making it a quieter option.</li>
          <li><strong>Pitadeniya:</strong> A less-used western entrance, accessible from the Neluwa–Udugama road. Best for those with a private vehicle.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>There is no direct public bus to the Kudawa entrance — the nearest bus stop is at Weddagala, from which you will need a three-wheeler. Most visitors arrive by private vehicle or as part of an organised tour.</p>

        <h2>Where to Stay Near Sinharaja</h2>
        <p>Accommodation near Sinharaja is limited. The village of Kudawa has a few basic guesthouses and homestays — staying here allows an early start (before 6am) that is essential for the best bird watching. The Forest Department also operates a basic rest house near the Kudawa entrance which can be booked in advance through their Colombo office. In Deniyaya, a small number of guesthouses cater to visitors approaching from the south. Most higher-end accommodation options require a 45–60 minute drive from the park boundary.</p>

